    <div class=3D"s-prose js-post-body" itemprop=3D"text">
<p>Regarding the <a href=3D"https://stackoverflow.com/a/41613532/1223975">h=
ttps://stackoverflow.com/a/41613532/1223975</a> solution that Alexander Mil=
ls reposted, <code>Timeout in Seconds</code> <code>Darwin</code>, is a work=
around for a UNIX-like OS that does not have GNU <code>tail</code>.  It is =
not specific to <code>Darwin</code>, but, depending on the age of the UNIX-=
like operating system, the command-line offered is more complex than necess=
ary, and can fail:</p>

<pre class=3D"lang-bash s-code-block"><code data-highlighted=3D"yes" class=
=3D"hljs language-bash">lsof -p <span class=3D"hljs-variable">$pid</span> +=
r 1m%s -t | grep -qm1 $(<span class=3D"hljs-built_in">date</span> -v+<span =
class=3D"hljs-variable">${timeout}</span>S +%s 2&gt;/dev/null || <span clas=
s=3D"hljs-built_in">echo</span> INF)
</code></pre>

<p>On at least one old UNIX, the <code>lsof</code> argument <code>+r 1m%s</=
code> fails (even for a superuser):</p>

<pre class=3D"lang-bash s-code-block"><code data-highlighted=3D"yes" class=
=3D"hljs language-bash">lsof: can<span class=3D"hljs-string">'t read kernel=
 name list.
</span></code></pre>

<p>The <code>m%s</code> is an output format specification.  A simpler post-=
processor does not require it.  For example, the following command waits on=
 PID 5959 for up to five seconds:</p>

<pre class=3D"lang-bash s-code-block"><code data-highlighted=3D"yes" class=
=3D"hljs language-bash">lsof -p 5959 +r 1 | awk <span class=3D"hljs-string"=
>'/^=3D/ { if (T++ &gt;=3D 5) { exit 1 } }'</span>
</code></pre>

<p>In this example, if PID 5959 exits of its own accord before the five sec=
onds elapses, <code>${?}</code> is <code>0</code>.  If not <code>${?}</code=
> returns <code>1</code> after five seconds.</p>

<p>It may also be worth expressly noting that in <code>+r 1</code>, the <co=
de>1</code> is the poll interval (in seconds), so it may be changed to suit=
 the situation.</p>
